{"id": "sevt_01Ets5832Q6Z4JHQko8vrABZ", "content": [{"text": "Confirmed. The per-row `order_items` queries appear only starting at 14:46:08 (none before the deploy), many fired under a single `request_id` \u2014 textbook N+1.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- **Deploy:** `ccf7a0f` (checkout, `frank@shop.io`) landed 14:46:05 UTC \u2014 \"refactor order summary builder for readability.\"\n- **Metric:** checkout p99 flat at ~70\u201380ms through 14:45, jumps to 3006ms at 14:46 and holds 3000\u20134300ms. Error rate stays ~0 (requests succeed, just slow).\n- **Diff:** `build_summary` replaced one batched query (`OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id__in=ids)`, grouped in memory) with a per-order `OrderItem.objects.filter(order_id=order.order_id)` inside the `for order in rows` loop.\n- **Log:** `SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = ?` per-row queries first appear 14:46:08 (none earlier), 118\u2013162/min, many sharing a single `request_id` \u2014 one query per order instead of one per request.\n\nThis is the top item on the runbook's \"things that have burned us\" list (per-row query where there used to be a batch). Fix: restore the batched `order_id__in` fetch + in-memory grouping; roll back `ccf7a0f` to stop the bleeding.\n\n> **Root cause:** `ccf7a0f` \u2014 the order-summary refactor turned a single batched `order_id__in` query into a per-order query inside the loop (N+1), driving checkout p99 from ~75ms to ~4s.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T05:41:11.865658Z", "type": "agent.message"}
